**Clarify.** Is the search recursive? What file attributes exist (name, extension, size, created/modified date)? Should results be a list or streamed? Are filters combined with nested logic like `(type=pdf AND size>1MB) OR modified>date`?

**Model.**
- `File` (or `FileNode`): name, extension, size, createdAt, modifiedAt, path. A `Directory` holds children (files and sub-directories).
- `Filter` interface: `boolean matches(File file)`.
- Concrete filters: `TypeFilter(ext)`, `DateFilter(from, to)`, `SizeFilter(min, max)`, `NameFilter(pattern)`. Each is small and independent.

**Combining filters (Composite).**
- `AndFilter(List<Filter>)`: matches only if all children match; short-circuit on the first false.
- `OrFilter(List<Filter>)`: matches if any child matches; short-circuit on the first true.
- Optionally `NotFilter(Filter)`.
Because composites are themselves `Filter`s, they nest to any depth with no extra code.

**Search.**
```java
List<File> search(Directory dir, Filter f) {
  List<File> out = new ArrayList<>();
  for (Node n : dir.children()) {
    if (n instanceof Directory d) out.addAll(search(d, f));
    else if (f.matches((File) n)) out.add((File) n);
  }
  return out;
}
```
A `FileSearcher` class owns this method so the filter and traversal concerns stay separate.

**Complexity.** O(N * F) for N files and F filter evaluations per file (less with short-circuiting). Space is O(depth) for recursion plus the result list.

**Extensibility.** New criterion means a new class implementing `Filter`; no change to the searcher (open/closed). A builder or fluent helper (`Filters.type("pdf").and(Filters.size(...))`) makes composition readable.

**Mistakes to avoid.** One method with many `if` flags, hardcoding AND-only logic, and ignoring empty filter lists (decide that an empty AND matches everything).
